===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# An [[increase]] in [[size]], [[number]], [[value]], or [[strength]].
# {{lb|en|economics}} {{ellipsis of|en|economic growth}}.
#: {{ux|en|'''Growth''' was dampened by a softening of the global economy in 2001, but picked up in the subsequent years due to strong '''growth''' in China.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-22|volume=407|issue=8841|page=70|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=Engineers of a different kind|url=http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21579879-buy-out-firm-really-does-focus-operational-improvements-engineers|passage=Private-equity nabobs bristle at being dubbed mere financiers.{{...}}Much of their pleading is public-relations bluster. Clever financial ploys are what have made billionaires of the industry’s veterans. “Operational improvement” in a portfolio company has often meant little more than promising colossal bonuses to sitting chief executives if they meet ambitious '''growth''' targets. That model is still prevalent today.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/oct/05/liz-truss-says-she-wants-growth-growth-and-growth-in-protest-hit-speech-tory-conference|quotee=Liz Truss|author=Rowena Mason|title=Liz Truss promises ‘growth, growth and growth’ in protest-hit speech|date=2022-10-05|work=The Guardian|passage=Liz Truss has promised Britons she has “got your back” and set out a plan for “'''growth''', '''growth''' and '''growth'''” in a conference speech disrupted by protesters asking who voted for her plan.}}
# An increase in psychological strength or resilience; an increased ability to overcome adversity.
#: {{ux|en|Struggle, disappointment, and criticism all contribute to a person's '''growth'''.}}
# {{lb|en|biology}} The act of [[growing]], getting [[bigger]] or [[higher]].
# {{lb|en|biology}} Something that [[grow]]s or has [[grown]].
# {{lb|en|pathology}} An [[abnormal]] [[mass]] such as a [[tumor]].

